ext logical  step in military development especially  since developed nations are increasingly  reliant on satellites and therefore  increasingly vulnerable to foreign  attacks another thing people get wrong  about militarizing space is that they  assume the outer space treaty prohibits  weaponizing space vessels this is only  partly correct the treaty which the US  UK Russia and a hundred and four other  nations have signed prohibits the  placing of nuclear weapons anywhere in  space it also prohibits building  military bases testing weapons or  conducting military maneuvers on any  celestial body however the treaty does  not prohibit the use of  weapons which are still deadly in fact  Russia has already sent at least one  serious weapon to space in the dark  depths of the Cold War the Soviet Union  secretly built and installed a 14.5  millimeter cannon that could reportedly  fire between 950 and 5,000 rounds per  minute and accurately hit targets up to  2 miles away  of course the tremendous recoil  generated by the cannon would have made  it hard to use effectively in space  requiring the use of thrusters to  counteract its momentum and the entire  20-ton space station had to be rotated  to line up their shot the weapon was  only tested once just before deorbiting  the space station other more effective  conventional weapons would include  kinetic bombardment rods  telephone-pole-sized cylinders of  tungsten which could be aided by a  rudimentary guidance system to hit  targets on earth striking with enough  kinetic energy to do massive damage even  without explosives the problem with this  type of weapon is that it would have to  be launched from a satellite which would  have to be positioned slowly and  precisely into an orbit that would  intersect with the intended target which  could take a very long time much longer  than say a ground-based ICBM at this  point we really don't have much in the  way of space weaponry that would be more  effective than earth-based tools but  that doesn't mean we won't see space  weapons in the near future these will  most likely be basic defenses for  satellites but if countries start  pulling out of the outer space treaty we  should be concerned  so these militarizing space a good thing  or a bad thing well we have to look at  it rationally sure space force might  sound silly and it might be kind of  disappointing to think that outer space  could become just another battlefield  rather than a purely scientific domain  but we have to realize there are some  very positive possibilities first we  know that Russia and North Korea have  been incredibly antagonistic towards the  Western world being able to defend our  satellites from foreign adversaries is  absolutely necessary to prevent a  crippling of Western nations and a  collapse of communication and navigation  systems another important consideration  is that any time the military gets  involved in research and development for  a new challenge the technology  inevitably trickles down to the average  consumer in this instance with the  military pouring huge amounts of money  into developing space technology if NASA  isn't going to get a budget raised  anytime soon this is the next best thing  new propulsion technologies guidance  systems life-support communications the  possibilities are endless and could  easily be applied to purely scientific  pursuits the military being involved in  could also inspire huge numbers of young  people to pursue a career in aerospace  engineering or astrophysics or any  number of important fields that could  fill roles in both the military and the  public sector and perhaps even rekindle  enough interest to spark a new space  race if this new space force really  wants to be useful they can spend their  time tracking non-military threats in  addition to foreign interference giant  asteroids pose a huge threat to our  existence and could easily wipe us off  the face of the earth in one random  collision the 